.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS open
.EX
Db *open(u8[] dbname, bool printerror=false);
.EE
.PP
Open (or create) the database file dbname, ":memory:" for a private in-memory one. null on failure, printing sqlite's message first when printerror is true. The Db keeps its own copy of dbname.
.SS close
.EX
void close(Db *ctx);
.EE
.PP
Close the database and free ctx. Statements not finalized yet keep the connection open until sql.finalize. null is ignored.
.SS prepare
.EX
Stmt *prepare(Db *ctx, u8[] sql);
.EE
.PP
Compile one SQL statement with \fB?\fR parameters, to run many times with sql.execPrepared. Only whitespace and comments may follow it. null on an error; free it with sql.finalize.
.SS finalize
.EX
void finalize(Stmt *pstmt);
.EE
.PP
Free a statement of sql.prepare. null is ignored.
.SS execRaw
.EX
String execRaw(Db *ctx, u8[] sql);
.EE
.PP
Run every statement in sql, with no parameters (so never with values from outside the program). An empty String on success, otherwise the error message as a new String (yours).
.SS execPrepared
.EX
i32 execPrepared(Stmt *pstmt, Row *row, Param *params);
.EE
.PP
Run a prepared statement with params[0] .. params[n - 1] for its n parameters (null params binds them all to null). Returns sqlite's code: SQLITE_ROW when row now holds the first row (read the rest with sql.nextPrepared), SQLITE_DONE when there are no rows, or an error: SQLITE_MISUSE for a null pstmt, SQLITE_MISMATCH for a parameter type other than SQL_INT/FLOAT/TEXT/BLOB/null, or the code of a failed bind (e.g. SQLITE_TOOBIG). On an error the statement does not run and row holds no row. The row belongs to the statement: it is valid until the next sql.execPrepared or sql.finalize.
.SS select
.EX
bool select(Db *ctx, Row *row, u8[] sql, Param *params=null, i32 paramCount=0);
.EE
.PP
Run one SQL statement with param_count parameters and point row at its first row, if any; read the rows with sql.next. true if the statement ran (also with no rows), false on an error, an unknown parameter type or anything but whitespace and comments after the statement. The row owns the statement: sql.next frees it after the last row, sql.rowRelease when you stop early.
.SS exec
.EX
bool exec(Db *ctx, u8[] sql, Param *params=null, i32 paramCount=0);
.EE
.PP
Like sql.select, for a statement whose rows you don't need (INSERT, UPDATE, CREATE, ...).
.SS next
.EX
i32 next(Row *row);
.EE
.PP
Move row to its next row (the first one on the first call): true while there is one, false after the last, when the row is released.
.SS nextPrepared
.EX
i64 nextPrepared(Row *row);
.EE
.PP
sql.next for a row of sql.execPrepared (the same, but returns i64).
.SS rowRelease
.EX
void rowRelease(Row *row);
.EE
.PP
Release a row before its last row was read: free its columns and, for a row of sql.select, its statement. A row of sql.execPrepared leaves its statement to sql.finalize. Calling it again does nothing.
